<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Vicente Gómez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Vicente Gómez was a soldier from the Andes who seized power in Venezuela in 1908 and ruled as dictator until his death in 1935. His regime crushed opposition, centralised the state and built a professional army. Oil was discovered under his rule, and through concessions to foreign companies Venezuela became one of the world's leading oil exporters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez.jpg" alt="Juan Vicente Gómez" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, Restored version by Wilfredor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B3mez,_1928_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Vicente Gómez Chacón was born on 24 July 1857, the firstborn son of Pedro Cornelio Gómez and Hermenegilda Chacón Alarcón, in a prominent family of Andean landowners at La Mulera. In 1899 he joined the private army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro">Cipriano Castro</a>, a friend since Castro's exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, which swept down on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> that year and seized control of the country. He became Castro's second vice president in 1901 and first vice president in 1904, and in 1902 head of the military, responsible for putting down several major revolts, among them the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-bolivar">Ciudad Bolívar</a> on 21 July 1903. He seized power from Castro in a coup on 19 December 1908, while Castro was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for medical treatment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Gómez reduced Venezuela's heavy debt by granting concessions to foreign oil companies after petroleum was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a> in 1914. This won him the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and brought economic stability. He launched a large public works programme that included the Transandean Highway, 1,539 km long, and founded the country's first airline and the Venezuelan Air Force, while the army was reorganised on a modern basis. He also received generous kickbacks and became by far the richest man in the country. Congress gave him the title El Benemérito, the Meritorious One; opponents called him El Bagre, the Catfish, and the Tyrant of the Andes.</p><p>He officially held the presidency on only three occasions. Re-elected in 1914, he declined to take office, and Victorino Márquez served as provisional president while Gómez ruled from Maracay. He returned to office in 1922 and ruled until 22 April 1929, when Juan Bautista Pérez became president with Gómez as the final authority. On 13 June 1931 Congress forced Pérez to resign and elected Gómez again, and this time he resumed office, holding it until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Students of the Generation of 1928, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulo-betancourt">Rómulo Betancourt</a> and Jóvito Villalba, led protests against him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>. His secret police were ubiquitous: Time magazine wrote that he kept twelve spies on his payroll for every policeman in Caracas. Gómez held basic civil liberties in disdain and did little for public education.</p><p>Supporters credit him with ending the civil wars and the power of regional caudillos, repaying all foreign and internal debt, and, through roads and oil-industry jobs, spreading a sense of national unity. Critics, including Betancourt, called him an instrument of foreign control of the Venezuelan economy, since Royal Dutch Shell and Standard Oil gained exploration rights under him. Juan Vicente Gómez International Airport was named for him in 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Vicente Gómez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Civil wars and revolts continued until Cipriano Castro and <b>Juan Vicente Gómez</b>, leaders from the Andean state of Táchira, seized Caracas in 1899.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in Maracay, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.83,-72.08&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Táchira on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juan Vicente Gómez"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3984 4332h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3984 4332h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=7.83,-72.08&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
